 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of samples per ray and the number of volumes. Adaptive sampling (`AutoAdjustSampleDistances`) can reduce samples for distant or simple regions, improving performance [S2, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the size of the 3D textures for all volumes. Limited by `MaxMemoryInBytes` [S2, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** Hardware-accelerated via fragment shaders, leveraging GPU texture sampling hardware for trilinear interpolation [S1, unofficial]. Performance is dependent on GPU memory bandwidth and shader execution units.
-- **Strengths:** Supports simultaneous rendering of multiple volumes with different transfer functions; efficient use of GPU hardware; supports adaptive sampling and early ray termination [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Limited to a small number of additional volumes (up to 4 extra) due to shader uniform limits and loop constraints; non-zero extents in input data require workarounds (origin/extent adjustment) [S2, unofficial]; `ReportProgress` can cause significant slowdowns if enabled for small datasets [S2, unofficial].
-- **When to Use:** When multiple volumes need to be visualized simultaneously with different properties, and hardware acceleration is available.
-- **When Not to Use:** When more than the maximum supported number of volumes are needed, or when running on hardware without sufficient OpenGL support or memory.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of samples per ray and the number of volumes. The ray marching loop iterates for each sample, and for each sample, it performs texture lookups and compositing for all $N$ volumes.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the sum of the memory required for $N$ 3D textures and $N$ 1D opacity textures. Limited by `MaxMemoryInBytes` [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Uses GLSL shaders, which are faster and more widely supported than OpenCL for this purpose [S1, unofficial]. Adaptive sampling (`AutoAdjustSampleDistances`) can improve performance by reducing sample density when render time is constrained [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simultaneous rendering of multiple volumes with correct depth ordering and compositing. Hardware-accelerated sampling and shading.
+*   **Limitations:** Hardware loop limits may require nested loop structures or careful unrolling [S1, unofficial]. Non-zero extents in input data require manual adjustment of origin and extents [S2, unofficial]. `ReportProgress` using `glFinish()` can significantly slow down rendering of small datasets if enabled [S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When visualizing multiple co-registered or spatially related volume datasets (e.g., multi-modal medical imaging) simultaneously.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When rendering a single volume (use `vtkGPUVolumeRayCastMapper` for better optimization), or when input data has more than one independent component (unsupported) [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The GPU Multi-Volume Ray Casting Mapper was developed as an extension to the Visualization Toolkit (VTK) to support multi-volume rendering on the GPU. It was modified by Carlos Falcón and Karl Krissian to extend the single-volume `vtkGPUVolumeRayCastMapper` [S1, unofficial]. The implementation uses GLSL shaders instead of OpenCL for better performance and broader device support [S1, unofficial]. It is part of the VTK volume rendering pipeline, building on the principles of direct volume ray casting and hardware-accelerated sampling [5616-hardware-accelerated-volume-rendering.md].
+The GPU Multi-Volume Ray Casting Mapper was developed as an extension to the Visualization Toolkit (VTK) to support multi-volume rendering on the GPU. It was modified by Carlos Falcón and Karl Krissian to extend the single-volume `vtkGPUVolumeRayCastMapper` [S1, unofficial]. The implementation relies on GLSL shaders for ray traversal and compositing, chosen for their speed and device support compared to OpenCL [S1, unofficial]. It is used in VTK-based applications requiring simultaneous visualization of multiple scalar fields.
 
